{
  "dom_id": "step-q47047",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /8)^{+}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x).$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's try direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /8)^{+}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /8)^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not defined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14258704/assets/60d8281e4b2655ebb054a54616b4e034be1bda60579db8b565346275d68acc11.png)\n\nWe see that as $x$ approaches $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x)$ are negative and decrease without bound. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /8)^{+}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x)=-\\infty .\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
